Q: Proper python type-hinting for functions that return a list of objects of unknown classes A: <p>One thing that is not clear from the code you posted is how a function such as <code>filter_medieval_items</code>, which takes an integer argument, creates the list it returns and what is in that list, although it seems likely that the list would contain a combination of <code>MedievalBook</code> and <code>MedievalDrawing</code> instances.</p>
<p><strong>It may be too late for you, but I would take a more object-oriented approach that would simplify your runtime logic</strong>. In class method <code>MuseumItem.filter_items</code> (see below), since I have no idea how candidates for filtering are obtained, I will just assume you make some call that returns a list of candidates that have been assigned to varibale <code>list_of_candidates</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>class MuseumItem:
    def __init__(self, museum, title, /, *args, **kwargs):
        self.museum = museum
        self.title = title
        ...  # Any other code to initialize a museum item

    @classmethod
    def filter_items(cls, museum: int) -&gt; list['MuseumItem']:
        # Code that returns candidate items
        candidates: list[MuseumItem] = some_list_of_candidates
        return [
            candidate for candidate in candidates
            if candidate.museum == museum and isinstance(candidate, cls)
        ]

    def __repr__(self):
        return f'{self.__class__.__name__}({self.museum!r}, {self.title!r})'

class Book(MuseumItem):
    def __init__(self, museum, title, /, *args, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(museum, title,  *args, **kwargs)
        ...  # Any book-specific initialization

class Drawing(MuseumItem):
    def __init__(self, museum, title, /, *args, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(museum, title,  *args, **kwargs)
        self.is_drawing = True
        ...  # Any drawing-specific initialization

class MedievalItem(MuseumItem):
    def __init__(self, museum, title, /,  *args, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(museum, title,  *args, **kwargs)
        ...  # Any medieval-related initialization

class RenaissanceItem(MuseumItem):
    def __init__(self, museum, title, /,  *args, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(museum, title,  *args, **kwargs)
        ...  # Any rennaisance-related initialization

class MedievalBook(Book, MedievalItem):
    def __init__(self, museum, title, /, *args, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(museum, title,  *args, **kwargs)

class MedievalDrawing(Drawing, MedievalItem):
    def __init__(self, museum, title, /,  *args, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(museum, title,  *args, **kwargs)

class RenaissanceBook(Book, RenaissanceItem):
    def __init__(self, museum, title, /, *args, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(museum, title,  *args, **kwargs)

class RenaissanceDrawing(Drawing, RenaissanceItem):
    def __init__(self, museum, title, /, *args, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(museum, title,  *args, **kwargs)
</code></pre>
<p>With the above class definitions, function <code>items_exhibited</code> becomes:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code># For demo purposes:
some_list_of_candidates = [
    RenaissanceBook(1, 'A History of the Renaissance Part 1'),
    RenaissanceBook(1, 'A History of the Renaissance Part 2'),
    RenaissanceBook(1, 'A Shorter History of the Renaissance'),
    MedievalDrawing(1, 'Some Medieval Drawing'),
    RenaissanceBook(2, 'A History of the Renaissance Part 1'),
    RenaissanceBook(2, 'A History of the Renaissance Part 2'),
    MedievalDrawing(2, 'Some Medieval Drawing'),
    MedievalBook(2, 'Some Medieval Book'),
]

def items_exhibited(museum: int, cls: type[MuseumItem]) -&gt; list[MuseumItem]:
    return cls.filter_items(museum)

for item_exhibited in items_exhibited(2, MedievalItem):
    print(item_exhibited)
</code></pre>
<p>Prints:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>MedievalDrawing(2, 'Some Medieval Drawing')
MedievalBook(2, 'Some Medieval Book')
</code></pre>

Q: How do the left shifting operator preserve sign? A: <p>In Java Left shift operator (&lt;&lt;) while shifting bits towards left discard the sign bit and add 0 at the rightmost end</p>
<p>You can try playing with below Java code and the examples taken here will clear all your doubts about left shift operator</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>int num = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
System.out.format(&quot;%3d = %s\n&quot;, num, Integer.toBinaryString(num));
// -2147483648 = 10000000000000000000000000000000

num = (Integer.MIN_VALUE &lt;&lt; 1);
System.out.format(&quot;%1d = %s\n&quot;, num, Integer.toBinaryString(num));
// 0 = 00000000000000000000000000000000

num = -2004318072;
System.out.format(&quot;%1d = %s\n&quot;, num, Integer.toBinaryString(num));
// -2004318072 = 10001000100010001000100010001000

num = (-2004318072 &lt;&lt; 1);
System.out.format(&quot;%1d = %s\n&quot;, num, Integer.toBinaryString(num));
// 286331152 = 00010001000100010001000100010000
</code></pre>
<p>This is just only for Java and I don't know about other languages. But you can use examples from above and try in other languages</p>

Q: How do you make a Button conditionally hidden or disabled? A: <p>Here is a View extension that will avoid conditional views and should not affect animations adversely (avoids if statements):</p>
<pre><code>extension View {
    @ViewBuilder
    func hide(_ condition: @autoclosure () -&gt; Bool) -&gt; some View {
        let size: CGSize? = condition() ? .zero : nil
        frame(width: size?.width, height: size?.height)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This can be converted from a <code>@ViewBuilder</code> to a regular function but it is less readable and evaluates the condition <code>@autoclosure</code> twice:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>extension View {
    func hide(_ condition: @autoclosure () -&gt; Bool) -&gt; some View {
        frame(width: condition() ? 0 : nil,
              height: condition() ? 0 : nil)
    }
}
</code></pre>
